# Belonging to [[she|her]] (belonging to that female person or animal, or in poetic or old-fashioned language that ship, city, season, etc).
#: ''This is '''her''' book''
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|title=The Journal of the American Dental Association|page=765
|passage=Prodigal in everything, summer spreads '''her''' blessings with lavish unconcern, and waving '''her''' magic wand across the landscape of the world, she bids the sons of men to enter in [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1932|year_published=1965|author=w:Delos W. Lovelace|title=[[w:King Kong (1933 film)|King Kong]]|page=1|passage=Her crew knew that deep in '''her''' heart beat engines fit and able to push her blunt old nose ahead at a sweet fourteen knots, come Hell or high water.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2001
|author=Betsy Gould Hearne
|title=Wishes, Kisses, and Pigs
|publisher=Simon and Schuster
|isbn=9780689841224
|page=78
|passage=On top of the circle she wrote '''her''' name, Louise, just above where the 12 on a clock would be.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Andrew Lambert|title=Nelson: Britannia's God of War|publisher=Faber & Faber|isbn=9780571265701
|passage=On 24 April Nelson rejoined his ship, '''her''' battle damage repaired {{...}}}}
# Belonging to a person of unspecified gender {{q|to counterbalance the traditional "his" in this sense}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|author=David Yellin|title=Essentials of Integrating the Language Arts|page=115
|passage=Begin by having students choose a short poem to memorize; they will enjoy searching the library for a poem that appeals to them. If a student wishes to memorize '''her''' poem and share it aloud with the rest of the class, suggest a buddy system.}}

